      "suite": "ai-home",
      "description": "Drones are the eyes, a detached rolling six-axis arm is the hands, the swarm plans and you hold command — and the machine knows only what its sensors have seen. This is the simulation environment for that machine: the room starts entirely unknown; a base LiDAR and a mini drone's LiDAR map it into a 3-D occupancy grid (the drone exploring frontier to frontier), surfaces and objects are discovered from the map alone, plans are drafted against those discovered ids and rehearsed on a clone, you execute behind a confirm, every kinetic step is re-validated live against the map (unknown space blocks everything), the wrist camera looks before and after each grasp, the drone re-scans to verify, and a 3-D view shows the mapped voxels, the arm model and the simulated pictures. The drone can go first and blind: its upward ranger clears the climb, its pose is an estimate that drifts by a deterministic odometry model, and every sweep is registered scan-to-map (point-to-plane against the exact hit points the map kept) before it is integrated — a 10 cm error comes back under a millimetre, a registration that will not converge grounds the drone, the pad re-anchors it, and a true strike on something unmapped puts it down. The drone carries a chosen SENSOR SET: the 3-D LiDAR we would buy, or the 2-D ring plus downward depth camera of the drone we print — which flies in its own scan plane, holds altitude on a nadir ranger, registers in the plane against walls at any height, and explores by where its camera will learn the most. The drone we print is also a PARTS MODEL: sizing by momentum theory at the summed mass budget, every printed part a CAD Studio program (open it there for STEP, STL and drawings), the bought parts with approximate prices — the hardware design's tables are generated from it. A taught fridge door the arm pulls open against a 60 N seal is kept. The drone's TRUE pose can run on a physics plant: a package-owned MuJoCo engine container flies the printed drone's parts model (MJCF generated from it) under gusts with real contacts, and every guard, the map and the registration are unchanged; the first training task (hover and one leg) ships with its PID baseline, and a trained policy may fly the plant only after its recorded flight has passed this world's guards — the certification gate. The engine container is also a node on the swarm rail: it heartbeats in the way a drone node does and a world can run on it, so a real drone node slots in behind the same primitives. Named hidden scenes (the kitchen, a studio) are plain data the discovery never reads; a drone that reports itself lost recovers by a global search and its registered trail back to the pad. Deterministic, no hardware — every view says simulated.",
